<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19869844</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2131961</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>STUDIES ON PARATYPHOID C BACILLI ISOLATED IN CHINA</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Tenbroeck</surname><given-names>Carl</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>C. P.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Y&#x000fc;</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1c01bb0N0x1d9b240">From the Department of Pathology, Peiping (Peking) Union Medical College, Peiping, China, and the Department of Animal Pathology of The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research, Princeton, N. J.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>28</day><month>2</month><year>1931</year></pub-date><volume>53</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>307</fpage><lpage>315</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>14</day><month>11</month><year>1930</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1931, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> A brief clinical history of five patients from whom paratyphoid C was isolated and a description of the cultures are given. It is pointed out that the purely non-specific forms are more nearly related to the hog cholera bacillus than are those containing both specific and non-specific forms. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>